          "Risk" is the operative word, here, but it's not terribly
          quantifiable for the individual.  Human parameters cover a
          wide range, which is one thing that makes biostatistics
          challenging.  I assess my risks as fairly low.  My four
          grandparents - two still going - have attained a collective
          age of over 350 years, and they're all unreconstructed
          Southerners who'd think nothing of making three meals a day
          off pork products and see vegetables largely as a vehicle
          for fatback.  One grandmother died of cardiac arrest a year
          ago; she was 95.  My cholesterol was 194 the last time it
          was measured; that was back when I ate bacon and eggs seven
          breakfasts/week.  If I feel a crushing pain in my chest in
          twenty years (knock wood) it's more likely to be a steering
          column.

          Again, people are awfully complex systems, and the history
          of medicine suggests that therapeutic fads didn't go out
          with leeches.  Most of them had some grounding - even
          leeches - they just weren't The Answer as touted.  I doubt
          there is an Answer, but I don't doubt that informed opinion
          will change, and change again with increasing data and, yes,
          changes in intellectual fashion.  Woody Allen was being
          arch, but not too arch, in the defrosting scene at the
          beginning of _Sleeper_: " Health food?  Where were the
          steaks? Was there no hot fudge?  Was there no deep fat?"

          But I digress... _Chacun 'a son_ HDL's, eh?


