Date: Wed, 27 Jan 1993 10:50:36 -0800
From: fnerd@smds.com (FutureNerd Steve Witham)
Subject: short and long term capitalism

I wanted to make a couple short points about long- versus short-term
lookahead under capitalism.

David Jevans <jevans@apple.com> writes:

>Visionary products don't get
>built by people who are there to maximize profit for investors.

Are you saying that John Skulley isn't there to maximize profit, or that 
he isn't one of the builders?  I'd sort of agree with the latter, but 
then, the builders in the technical sense don't always create the companies 
that allow them to work.  I wouldn't say Jobs et. al. were profit-blind 
either.

Anyway,

The distance into the future that individuals in capitalism are 
thinking is not the same as the distance into the future that the
system as a whole is anticipating.  Your heart is anticipating its
next beat.  Your nerves are charging up for the next ten miliseconds.
These short-term activities are necessary for your long-term life.
In the same way, executives and investors may be thinking about 
the next trade, the next quarter, whatever.  But these are just the
heartbeats and nerve impulses.  The system as a whole is geared to 
the future.  It's like what Adam Smith said about selfishness.
The invisible hand aligns short-term incentives along long-term lines
of force.

On the other hand, individuals in a free market are not *required*
to think about money, stockholders and the short term.  (One way in
which our situation is not free is that corporations are required
to let the stockholders vote the board of directors.)  Anyway, 
people who are vision- not profit-driven are important to capitalism
--if they succeed!  And if they don't, what do they care, they weren't
there to maximize profit anyway, right?

-fnerd
