From: pmetzger@shearson.com (Perry E. Metzger)
Subject: ECON: Futures vs. Equities

> From: Robin Hanson <hanson@ptolemy.arc.nasa.gov>
> 
> I do not know what Perry means by "zero-sum", and he has so far chosen
> not to be explicit. But I think the issue of whether or not "bets"
> are worse than other investments like stocks because they are
> "zero-sum" is very important, and so will explain for the benefit of
> the rest of you.

I mean the same thing anyone using game theory terminology means when
they say "zero sum".

A "zero sum" game is a game in which there are a fixed number of points,
chips, dollars, or whatever, at the start of the game, and in which at the
end only the allocation of the points has changed. An example of a zero
sum game is poker. When five people sit down to play poker, they each
have a pool of dollars that they start with -- at the end of the game the sum
of those pools is the same as at the beginning, although the allocation
may have radically changed.

A "positive sum" game is a game in which the total number of points at the
end is greater than the number at the start.

In a bet, my loss is your gain, my gain is your loss. Money changes hands, but
no value is created. this is NOT to say that I consider bets to be valueless.
I believe that Robin is getting the completely incorrect opinion that I don't
like zero sum games somehow. Insurance, futures markets, options markets and
all the rest are very valuable. That does not mean, however, that they
aren't zero sum. By definition, if I make a contract with an insurance company
either they are going to be net winners because I don't lose my ship at
sea, or I am going to be a net winner because I did. I choose to play the
game even though its likely I'll lose money at it because its a small amount
of money and it keeps the variance on my return from my overall shipping
business low. They accept the risk because they tend to make money. I think
this is a perfectly valuable exchange, but it is ZERO SUM.

The equities market is positive sum. If I own 100 shares of GE and they
rise, I've made money but no one else has lost any. On the other hand,
if I am in the options market and I buy calls at 70 on GE and GE rises to
100, I've made $30 a contract, but similarly the guy writing the contract
LOST $30 a contract. Zero sum.

